voice Kyri
host Linux pier, America/New_York one clock
title The row repaired before its shelf froze
prompt take the Grass baton, open the round, close what is red, and send the lap whole
think Reds first: the newest hot transcript closed run_verdict=guard_red naming index_row_bound, so the lap re-ran that scan rather than trusting a day-old reading.
think The scan answered rows_over=1 on session-logs/date/README-index-20260914.md -- a 196-byte row against the 192 an index row is given -- and it derives its open shelf as the newest README-index-<day> BY NAME.
think That derivation means the day's first lap to write today's shelf closes yesterday's page to the guard, so anything standing over the bound at that moment is frozen over it while the roster reads green.
think The lap trimmed the row, then the round-open fetch read commits_behind=6 and a peer had trimmed the same row in afff49795; the local trim was dropped on the rebase, and the page records both hands.
obs The window closed at 17:46:17, when 3e7c10952 created session-logs/date/README-index-20260915.md; the trim stands in afff49795, the commit before it in history. About ninety seconds of margin, and the margin was luck.
obs Census over every day shelf: 26 shelves before 20260830 carry 1,569 rows above the bound, and the 15 shelves from 20260830 forward carry 1,207 rows with zero over. The day-boundary window has never once been paid.
obs The loom this lantern wants already stands -- index_row_bound is rostered and refused correctly both firings. The lap that met the first shipped YELLOW and read the refusal as weather, which is the layer that slipped.
obs tools/s/session_logs_index_prepend.rish writes the elder bullet form into the living pin, which has held no lap row since 20260827.171500, and no living file calls it. Every shelf row here is typed by a hand.
obs The living pin and CHAPTERS both still called 20260914 open while its shelf stood beside a 20260915 shelf upstream; both rosters now carry 104 for that day and an open row for today.
obs construction/ITINERARY.md stands at 40,425 bytes against its 40,960 bound -- 535 bytes of headroom with eight ships each writing an account. The next lap to add one meets the wall.
loom index_row_bound rows=2 rows_over=0 row_max=192 longest_row=181 open_shelf=20260915
loom shelf_census shelves_since_20260830=15 rows=1207 rows_over=0 shelves_before=26 rows_over_before=1569
loom page sentences=48 negative_percent=20 register=80 reach=100 truth=100 composite=93 grade=A
rota lap 4917, row 2 -- Fire, the row that sees; cut and stop. Standfast's own sentence decided the lap: a red read as weather is a line that kept moving, and the stop had to come before the shelf closed.
file active-designing/20260915-174558_the-shelf-that-closes-before-the-guard-looks-again.md records the day-boundary window, the commit order, the two-span census, and one proposed disposition
file session-logs/README.md closes 20260914 at 104 rows and opens today's row
file session-logs/CHAPTERS.md carries the same derived count, held by session_roster_agree
file construction/ITINERARY.md carries the Grass account and the ten-hex parent nib
recommend check in (Keaton) -- the disposition is one baton line naming the day's first act, and the card's 535 bytes of headroom wants a fold before the next account
scope close the standing index_row_bound reading, record the window that would have hidden it, and reconcile the two session rosters
status GREEN -- index_row_bound, session_roster_agree, remember_git_nib, index_fold, dayshelf_merge, log_has_a_row, ascii_document, two_rooms_doorway, and qa_report_card all GREEN on metal; the full cold roster pass is in flight at session-output/standing-equipment-cold.txt and is named rather than claimed
